window.NOOR_UNSEEN={"entries": [{"id": "jibril", "cat": "angels", "title": "Jibril, the Trustworthy Spirit", "ar": "جِبْرِيلُ", "hook": "The angel who carried every word of the Qur’an to the Prophet ﷺ was seen in his true form exactly twice.", "body": ["The Qur’an names him directly: say, whoever is an enemy to Jibril, it is he who brought the revelation down upon your heart by Allah’s permission (Qur’an 2:97). Surah al-Shu’ara calls the same carrier al-Ruh al-Amin, the Trustworthy Spirit, who descended with it upon the heart of the Prophet ﷺ in a clear Arabic tongue (Qur’an 26:193-195). Jibril alayhis-salam is not a symbol or a literary device. He is the appointed messenger between Allah and His prophets, entrusted with the words on which everything else in this religion stands.", "Surah al-Najm describes the one who taught the Prophet ﷺ as one of mighty power, who stood plainly on the highest horizon, then drew near (Qur’an 53:5-10). The mainstream position of the commentators, resting on the explanations of Aisha and Ibn Mas’ud in the Sahih collections, is that this figure is Jibril. Those same narrations state that the Prophet ﷺ saw him in the form in which he was created only twice, once filling the horizon and once at the Lote Tree of the furthest limit, and that he has six hundred wings (Bukhari 3232, Muslim 177, with Qur’an 53:13-18)."], "evidence": {"level": "quran", "claims": [{"text": "Jibril brings the revelation down upon the heart of the Prophet ﷺ by Allah’s permission", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 2:97", "Qur’an 26:193-195"]}, {"text": "The one of mighty power seen at the clear horizon in Surah al-Najm is Jibril", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Qur’an 53:5-10", "Bukhari 3232", "Muslim 177"]}, {"text": "The Prophet ﷺ saw Jibril in his true created form twice, and he has six hundred wings", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Qur’an 53:13-18", "Bukhari 3232", "Muslim 177"]}], "boundary": "The texts do not describe Jibril’s form beyond what they state, and they do not report anyone after the Prophet ﷺ seeing him as he was created.", "note": "A minority of early readings took the vision of Surah al-Najm differently, but the relayed explanations of Aisha and Ibn Mas’ud settled the mainstream position on Jibril."}, "refs": ["Qur’an 2:97", "Qur’an 26:193-195", "Qur’an 53:5-10", "Qur’an 53:13-18", "Bukhari 3232", "Muslim 177"]}, {"id": "mikail", "cat": "angels", "title": "Mikail, Named Beside Jibril", "ar": "مِيكَائِيلُ", "hook": "Only one ayah names Mikail, and it makes enmity toward him enmity toward Allah.", "body": ["Mikail alayhis-salam appears by name once in the Qur’an: whoever is an enemy to Allah, His angels, His messengers, Jibril, and Mikail, then Allah is an enemy to the disbelievers (Qur’an 2:98). The commentators relate that the ayah answered those who had declared Jibril their enemy, and its logic is sweeping: hostility toward any of Allah’s appointed envoys, angelic or human, is hostility toward Him. That a single verse sets Mikail beside Jibril in this way was enough for the scholars to count him among the greatest of the angels.", "As for his work, the description found across the tafsir tradition is that Mikail is charged with the rain and the vegetation, the measured provision that keeps every living thing fed. That level should be stated honestly: it comes from reports of the early generations, for example under Qur’an 2:98 in Ibn Kathir, not from an ayah or a rigorously authenticated hadith that spells it out. What the strongest sources do secure is his rank: the Prophet ﷺ opened his night prayer with, O Allah, Lord of Jibril, Mikail, and Israfil, Creator of the heavens and the earth (Muslim 770)."], "evidence": {"level": "mixed", "claims": [{"text": "Mikail is named in the Qur’an, and enmity toward him is counted as enmity toward Allah", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 2:98"]}, {"text": "The Prophet ﷺ opened his night prayer calling on Allah as Lord of Jibril, Mikail, and Israfil", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Muslim 770"]}, {"text": "Mikail is charged with the rain and the vegetation", "level": "debated", "refs": ["Tafsir Ibn Kathir 2:98"]}], "boundary": "Neither the Qur’an nor the strongest hadith spell out Mikail’s duties, so the rain and provision role rests on reports below that tier.", "note": "Many scholars transmit the provision role as received teaching while acknowledging it does not rest on an ayah or a rigorously authentic hadith."}, "refs": ["Qur’an 2:98", "Muslim 770", "Tafsir Ibn Kathir 2:98"]}, {"id": "israfil", "cat": "angels", "title": "Israfil and the Trumpet", "ar": "إِسْرَافِيلُ", "hook": "The blast that ends the world fills the Qur’an, yet the name of its blower reaches us by a different road.", "body": ["The Qur’an is explicit about the Sur, the Trumpet. It will be blown, and whoever is in the heavens and the earth will fall dead except whom Allah wills, then it will be blown again and all will be standing, looking on (Qur’an 39:68). Another ayah calls it a single blast (Qur’an 69:13), and a third shows mankind arriving in crowds on the Day it sounds (Qur’an 78:18). The event is certain and repeated across the revelation. What the Qur’an never does is name the angel whose task it is.", "The name arrives through the Sunnah and the scholars. The Prophet ﷺ called on Allah as Lord of Jibril, Mikail, and Israfil (Muslim 770), and an authentic hadith describes the companion of the Horn already holding it, waiting for the command to blow (Tirmidhi 2431). That this waiting angel is Israfil alayhis-salam is how the reports were understood across the generations; al-Qurtubi and Ibn Hajar relay it as the well-known, settled position, and some transmit consensus on it. So the honest order is: the blast is Qur’an, the blower’s name is hadith and scholarly agreement."], "evidence": {"level": "mixed", "claims": [{"text": "The Trumpet will be blown, all in the heavens and the earth will fall dead except whom Allah wills, and a second blast will raise them standing", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 39:68", "Qur’an 69:13", "Qur’an 78:18"]}, {"text": "Israfil is named in the Prophet’s ﷺ own supplication at night", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Muslim 770"]}, {"text": "The companion of the Horn has already taken it up and waits for the command to blow", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Tirmidhi 2431"]}, {"text": "The blower of the Trumpet is Israfil by name", "level": "debated", "refs": ["Ibn Hajar, Fath al-Bari", "al-Qurtubi, al-Tadhkirah"]}], "boundary": "The Qur’an itself never names the blower, and no source tells us when the blast will come.", "note": "The doubt recorded here concerns the chains of the explicit naming texts, not any live dispute among scholars over who the blower is."}, "refs": ["Qur’an 39:68", "Qur’an 69:13", "Qur’an 78:18", "Muslim 770", "Tirmidhi 2431", "al-Qurtubi, al-Tadhkirah"]}, {"id": "malak-almawt", "cat": "angels", "title": "The Angel of Death", "ar": "مَلَكُ الْمَوْتِ", "hook": "Revelation describes his task with perfect precision and never once gives him a name.", "body": ["Say, the angel of death who has been entrusted with you will take you, then to your Lord you will be returned (Qur’an 32:11). One angel, singular, appointed over the ending of every life. Other ayat widen the frame: Allah sends guardians over each servant until, when death comes to one of you, Our messengers take him, and they never fail (Qur’an 6:61), and the angels, plural, are described taking souls (Qur’an 4:97). The commentators reconcile these readings simply: the angel of death has angel assistants, and no soul’s appointment is ever missed.", "Then there is the name. Azrail fills stories, films, and even some classical pages, yet it occurs nowhere in the Qur’an and nowhere in any authentic hadith. Ibn Kathir states plainly that the angel of death is not named in the Qur’an or the sound narrations, and that the name Azrail comes only through some later reports, drawn largely from the lore of earlier communities (Ibn Kathir, al-Bidaya wa’l-Nihaya). Revelation calls him malak al-mawt, the angel of death, and a believer loses nothing by keeping exactly to the words revelation chose."], "evidence": {"level": "mixed", "claims": [{"text": "A single angel of death is entrusted with taking each soul at its appointed time", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 32:11"]}, {"text": "Angels in the plural also take souls, understood by the commentators as his assistants", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 6:61", "Qur’an 4:97"]}, {"text": "The angel of death is named Azrail", "level": "debated", "refs": ["Ibn Kathir, al-Bidaya wa’l-Nihaya"]}], "boundary": "The sources give this angel no personal name and no physical description.", "note": "Ibn Kathir traces the name Azrail to reports from earlier communities, which is why careful books simply say malak al-mawt."}, "refs": ["Qur’an 32:11", "Qur’an 6:61", "Qur’an 4:97", "Ibn Kathir, al-Bidaya wa’l-Nihaya"]}, {"id": "malik", "cat": "angels", "title": "Malik, Keeper of the Fire", "ar": "مَالِكٌ", "hook": "The Qur’an has already recorded the plea the people of the Fire will make to their keeper, and his answer.", "body": ["The Qur’an preserves a conversation that has not happened yet. And they will call out: O Malik, let your Lord make an end of us. He will say: you are remaining (Qur’an 43:77). One name, one office, recorded before the event. Around him stand the rest of the Fire’s custodians, angels stern and severe who do not disobey Allah in what He commands them (Qur’an 66:6), nineteen set over Saqar, and none but angels appointed its keepers (Qur’an 74:30-31). Malik alayhis-salam is no mythology; he is an obedient servant carrying out a command he will never exceed.", "The Sunnah confirms the name. In the long dream vision shown to the Prophet ﷺ, the one he saw kindling the Fire was identified to him as Malik, the keeper of Jahannam (Bukhari 7047). The Qur’an elsewhere records the people of the Fire pleading with its keepers, plural, to lighten one day of punishment, and being reminded that their messengers had already come with clear proofs (Qur’an 40:49-50, Qur’an 39:71). The scene in Surah al-Zukhruf then closes on Malik’s answer, quiet and final: you are staying."], "evidence": {"level": "quran", "claims": [{"text": "Malik is the keeper of the Fire, whom its people will address by name", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 43:77"]}, {"text": "The keepers of the Fire are angels, stern and severe, who never disobey Allah’s command", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 66:6", "Qur’an 74:30-31"]}, {"text": "The Prophet ﷺ was shown Malik, the keeper of Jahannam, in the dream vision narrated in the Sahih", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 7047"]}], "boundary": "Beyond his name, his office, and his obedience, the texts describe nothing of Malik, and they never show him acting on his own initiative.", "note": ""}, "refs": ["Qur’an 43:77", "Qur’an 66:6", "Qur’an 74:30-31", "Qur’an 39:71", "Qur’an 40:49-50", "Bukhari 7047"]}, {"id": "ridwan", "cat": "angels", "title": "The Keeper of Paradise and the Name Ridwan", "ar": "رِضْوَانُ", "hook": "That Paradise has keepers is revelation; the famous name Ridwan is a later inheritance.", "body": ["The Qur’an gives the scene. Those mindful of their Lord are led to Paradise in companies until, when they arrive and its gates are opened, its keepers say: peace be upon you, you have done well, so enter it to remain forever (Qur’an 39:73). Keepers, khazanah, welcoming rather than barring the way. The Sunnah adds a gate and a first opening: the Prophet ﷺ said that he will come to the gate of Paradise on the Day of Resurrection, and the keeper will ask who he is, and will say he was commanded to open for no one before him (Muslim 197).", "The name Ridwan is a separate matter. It became the standard label for the chief keeper of Paradise in later books, sermons, and poetry, and reports carrying it were gathered in works such as al-Suyuti’s collection on the angels, but it is not found in the Qur’an, nor in the strongest hadith collections. The office is revealed, the greeting is revealed, the name is inherited from reports below that rank. Saying the keeper of Paradise costs nothing and stays inside the evidence, which is exactly how talk of the unseen should behave."], "evidence": {"level": "mixed", "claims": [{"text": "Paradise has angel keepers who open its gates and greet its people with peace", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 39:73"]}, {"text": "A keeper will open the gate of Paradise first for the Prophet ﷺ and for no one before him", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Muslim 197"]}, {"text": "The chief keeper of Paradise is named Ridwan", "level": "debated", "refs": ["al-Suyuti, al-Haba’ik fi Akhbar al-Mala’ik"]}], "boundary": "No revealed text names the keeper of Paradise, so Ridwan remains a label from later reports rather than a point of established creed.", "note": "Some scholars allow Ridwan as widespread usage while noting its reports are weak, which is why careful books prefer the Qur’an’s own phrase, keepers of Paradise."}, "refs": ["Qur’an 39:73", "Muslim 197", "al-Suyuti, al-Haba’ik fi Akhbar al-Mala’ik"]}, {"id": "munkar-nakir", "cat": "angels", "title": "The Two Questioners of the Grave", "ar": "مُنْكَرٌ وَنَكِيرٌ", "hook": "That two angels will question you in the grave is firmly established; their famous names travel on a narrower road.", "body": ["The questioning itself stands on firm ground. The Prophet ﷺ described how, when a person is buried and his companions turn away, he hears their footsteps, and two angels come to him, sit him up, and ask him about his Lord, his religion, and the man who was sent among them (Bukhari 1338, Muslim 2870, and the long hadith graded sahih in Abu Dawud 4753). The Qur’an’s promise that Allah keeps firm those who believe with the firm word, in this life and in the Hereafter (Qur’an 14:27), was explained by the Prophet ﷺ as revealed about the grave (Bukhari 1369).", "The names are narrower. A narration in al-Tirmidhi calls the two angels of the grave al-Munkar and al-Nakir (Tirmidhi 1071); al-Tirmidhi graded it hasan gharib, and later specialists split over it, some accepting the naming as hasan and using it in creed texts, others judging the naming weak while affirming the questioning itself without hesitation. So the careful sentence runs in two registers: two angels will question you, by texts the ummah agrees are authentic, and they may bear these names, by a report whose grade is honestly contested."], "evidence": {"level": "mixed", "claims": [{"text": "Two angels question the buried person about his Lord, his religion, and the man sent among them", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 1338", "Muslim 2870", "Abu Dawud 4753"]}, {"text": "Allah keeps the believer firm with the firm word in the grave, as Qur’an 14:27 was explained", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Qur’an 14:27", "Bukhari 1369"]}, {"text": "The two questioners are named Munkar and Nakir", "level": "debated", "refs": ["Tirmidhi 1071"]}], "boundary": "The authentic narrations establish the questioning without giving the two angels names, and the naming report’s grade remains contested.", "note": "Many creed manuals keep the names Munkar and Nakir as received usage while resting the doctrine itself on the agreed narrations."}, "refs": ["Qur’an 14:27", "Bukhari 1338", "Bukhari 1369", "Muslim 2870", "Abu Dawud 4753", "Tirmidhi 1071"]}, {"id": "harut-marut", "cat": "angels", "title": "Harut and Marut, the Trial at Babylon", "ar": "هَارُوتُ وَمَارُوتُ", "hook": "One ayah carries their entire story, and it is stricter and cleaner than the legends built on top of it.", "body": ["Everything established about them sits inside one ayah, and it begins by clearing a prophet: Sulayman did not disbelieve, but the devils disbelieved, teaching people magic, and what was sent down upon the two angels at Babylon, Harut and Marut, who would teach no one until they had said: we are only a trial, so do not disbelieve (Qur’an 2:102). The ayah adds that people learned from them what separates a man from his wife, that no one is harmed by it except by Allah’s permission, and that whoever purchases it has no share in the Hereafter.", "Around that ayah later storytelling wrapped a famous tale of two angels who lusted, drank, killed, and hang punished in Babylon until the Hour. Ibn Kathir surveys those reports and concludes that none of it is authentically traceable to the Prophet ﷺ, and that its substance goes back to the transmitted lore of earlier communities (Tafsir Ibn Kathir 2:102). The Qur’an’s own account needs none of it: angels acting only as a trial decreed by Allah, a warning issued before every lesson, and the guilt resting where the ayah leaves it, on the one who buys."], "evidence": {"level": "mixed", "claims": [{"text": "Harut and Marut were two angels at Babylon, a trial from Allah, who warned every student before teaching anything", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 2:102"]}, {"text": "What was learned from them could separate spouses, yet harms no one except by Allah’s permission", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 2:102"]}, {"text": "The tale that the two angels sinned through wine, murder, and a woman is unestablished Israiliyyat", "level": "corrected", "refs": ["Tafsir Ibn Kathir 2:102"]}], "boundary": "The ayah records no sin, no punishment, and no romance of the two angels, and nothing beyond its own words is established.", "note": "Some early tafsir figures did relay versions of the tale, which is why Ibn Kathir addresses it at length before setting it aside as unestablished."}, "refs": ["Qur’an 2:102", "Tafsir Ibn Kathir 2:102"]}, {"id": "kiraman-katibin", "cat": "angels", "title": "Kiraman Katibin, the Noble Scribes", "ar": "كِرَامًا كَاتِبِينَ", "hook": "No word leaves your mouth without a noble scribe, already seated, writing it down.", "body": ["Over you are keepers, noble, writing, who know whatever you do (Qur’an 82:10-12). The Qur’an describes the arrangement with courtroom precision: when the two receivers receive, seated on the right and on the left, a person utters no word without a watcher beside him, ready (Qur’an 50:17-18). The scribes are called kiram, honored ones, and the record they keep is not surveillance for its own sake; it is the evidence of an accounting in which nothing is lost, nothing misheard, and nothing entered against the wrong name.", "The Sunnah shows mercy written into the rules of the ledger. The Prophet ﷺ related from his Lord that whoever intends a good deed and does not do it has a complete good deed written for him, and whoever does it has it written from ten times up to seven hundred and beyond, while an evil deed merely intended and left undone is written as a complete good deed, and an evil deed done is written as one (Bukhari 6491). The pen over your shoulder, in other words, is weighted toward forgiveness."], "evidence": {"level": "quran", "claims": [{"text": "Noble recording angels are stationed over every person and know what they do", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 82:10-12"]}, {"text": "Two receivers sit at the right and the left, and no word is spoken without a ready watcher", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 50:17-18"]}, {"text": "The recording multiplies good deeds and restrains the count of evil ones", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 6491"]}], "boundary": "The texts do not name the two scribes or describe their forms, and the record is not shown to anyone until the Day it is spread open.", "note": "Commentators often assign good deeds to the scribe of the right based on the ayah’s order and supporting reports rather than an explicit revealed statement."}, "refs": ["Qur’an 82:10-12", "Qur’an 50:17-18", "Bukhari 6491"]}, {"id": "hafaza", "cat": "angels", "title": "The Guardians in Succession", "ar": "الْحَفَظَةُ", "hook": "Angels guard you in relays, before and behind, until the moment the written decree arrives.", "body": ["For each person there are angels in succession, before him and behind him, guarding him by the command of Allah (Qur’an 13:11). The word is mu’aqqibat, ones who relieve one another in relays around a single human being. Another ayah completes the arc: He is the Subduer over His servants, and He sends guardians, hafazah, over you, until, when death comes to one of you, Our messengers take him, and they never fail (Qur’an 6:61). A life, in these two ayat, is escorted from beginning to end by appointed and unfailing custody.", "The Prophet ﷺ described one such rotation: angels of the night and angels of the day take turns among you, gathering at the dawn prayer and the afternoon prayer, and those who spent the night among you ascend, and their Lord asks them, though He knows best, how they left His servants, and they answer: we left them praying (Bukhari 555, Muslim 632). Ibn Abbas and the early commentators added the sober edge: the guardians shield a person by Allah’s command, and when the decree itself arrives, they make way for it."], "evidence": {"level": "quran", "claims": [{"text": "Angels in relays guard each person, before him and behind him, by Allah’s command", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 13:11"]}, {"text": "Guardians are sent over every servant until death, when the messengers take him without fail", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 6:61"]}, {"text": "Angels of the night and the day rotate among people and gather at the dawn and afternoon prayers", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 555", "Muslim 632"]}], "boundary": "The guarding is by Allah’s command and within His decree, and the texts never present it as immunity from harm.", "note": "Ibn Abbas explained that the guardians protect a person until the decree comes, and then they stand aside for it."}, "refs": ["Qur’an 13:11", "Qur’an 6:61", "Bukhari 555", "Muslim 632"]}, {"id": "usayd-lights", "cat": "angels", "title": "The One UFO Encounter in History With an Answer", "ar": "", "hook": "One night in Madinah, unidentified lights over a reciter of the Qur’an received what no modern sighting has: an identification.", "body": ["Usayd ibn Hudayr, may Allah be pleased with him, was reciting Surah al-Baqarah in the night, his horse tethered beside him, when the animal began to stir. He fell silent and it calmed; he resumed and it reared again. His son Yahya lay near the horse, so he stopped for the boy’s sake, looked up, and saw something like a canopy above him, filled with what looked like lamps, rising into the sky until he could no longer see it. In the morning he told the Prophet ﷺ, who told him: recite, Ibn Hudayr, recite, Ibn Hudayr (Bukhari 5018, Muslim 796).", "Then came the identification. Those, the Prophet ﷺ said, were the angels drawing near for your voice, and had you kept reciting until morning, the people would have looked at them; they would not have been hidden from them. Strictly speaking, a UFO is only what its letters claim: a flying object unidentified at the moment it is seen. By that plain definition, Usayd saw one, lights aloft that he could not name that night. What removes this sighting from the modern catalog is that it did not stay unidentified; the answer came from the Prophet ﷺ himself.", "The boundary matters as much as the wonder. This report identifies one night’s lights above one reciter in Madinah; it does not hand us a master key to every strange light since. Islam affirms unseen beings, angels and jinn, but no text teaches that modern aerial reports are those beings, and nothing here licenses that leap. What the account does teach is what drew the angels close: not spectacle, not summons, but the Book of Allah recited in the dark by a believer, so beautifully that the sky’s inhabitants came near to listen."], "evidence": {"level": "sunnah", "claims": [{"text": "While Usayd ibn Hudayr recited Surah al-Baqarah at night, a canopy like a cloud with what looked like lamps hovered above and withdrew when he stopped", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 5018", "Muslim 796"]}, {"text": "The Prophet ﷺ identified the lights as angels who drew near for the recitation", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 5018", "Muslim 796"]}, {"text": "Had he kept reciting until morning, the people would have seen them, unhidden", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 5018", "Muslim 796"]}, {"text": "Calling this a UFO encounter means only that the object was unidentified at the moment of sighting and, uniquely, was identified afterward", "level": "editorial", "refs": ["Bukhari 5018"]}], "boundary": "The report identifies one specific sighting and does not establish that modern UFO reports are angels, jinn, or anything else in particular.", "note": "A parallel narration about Surah al-Kahf names the descending presence as the sakinah, tranquility, so not every such descent is explained the same way."}, "refs": ["Bukhari 5018", "Muslim 796"]}, {"id": "jinn-creation", "cat": "jinn", "title": "Created from Smokeless Fire", "ar": "وَخَلَقَ الْجَانَّ مِن مَّارِجٍ مِّن نَّارٍ", "hook": "Before the first human breathed, another creation already walked the earth, made of something stranger than clay.", "body": ["The Qur’an states plainly that the jinn were created from fire: ’He created the jann from a smokeless flame of fire’ (55:15), and elsewhere, ’the jann We created before, from the fire of scorching wind’ (15:27). Two facts stand on these ayat. First, their substance: marij, a smokeless, mixing flame, and samum, a burning wind. Second, their timing: min qablu, before Adam. The jinn are not metaphors or psychological forces in the Qur’an’s telling. They are a creation, deliberate and dated, older than us.", "By default they are unseen: ’he sees you, he and his tribe, from where you see them not’ (7:27). Notice what the ayah grounds and what it does not. It grounds their reality and our blindness to them, not their omniscience or omnipresence. The believer holds both truths without drama: a parallel creation exists, and Allah has veiled it from our eyes in ordinary life. Fear is not the lesson here. Awareness is, and the ayah itself was revealed as a warning against Shaytan’s deception, not an invitation to obsess over the hidden."], "evidence": {"level": "quran", "claims": [{"text": "The jinn were created from a smokeless flame of fire", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 55:15", "Qur’an 15:27"]}, {"text": "The jinn were created before Adam", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 15:27"]}, {"text": "Jinn see humans while humans, in ordinary life, do not see them", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 7:27"]}], "boundary": "The texts do not describe the physics of the jinn’s substance, their appearance, or the details of where and how they live.", "note": "Most commentators read al-jann in these ayat as the jinn race or their forefather."}, "refs": ["Qur’an 55:15", "Qur’an 15:27", "Qur’an 7:27"]}, {"id": "jinn-free-will", "cat": "jinn", "title": "Believers and Rebels Among Them", "ar": "وَأَنَّا مِنَّا الصَّالِحُونَ وَمِنَّا دُونَ ذَٰلِكَ", "hook": "The Qur’an records a confession from the jinn themselves: some of us are righteous, and some of us are otherwise.", "body": ["Surah al-Jinn opens with an event, not a legend: a company of jinn heard the Qur’an recited and said, ’indeed we have heard an amazing recitation, guiding to right conduct, so we believed in it’ (72:1-2). The same surah lets them describe their own society: ’among us are the righteous, and among us are otherwise, we are ways divided’ (72:11), and ’among us are those who submit, and among us are the unjust’ (72:14). Belief, doubt, obedience, rebellion: the full moral range we know in ourselves.", "Their accountability rests on the same foundation as ours: ’I did not create the jinn and mankind except to worship Me’ (51:56). One purpose, two creations. This is why the surah ends with jinn facing the same outcomes we face, fuel for Jahannam for the unjust among them (72:15) and a right path for those who submit. The jinn are not props in human stories. They are a tested community, and the fair conclusion is neither fascination nor dread but recognition: unseen neighbours under the same Lord, sitting the same exam."], "evidence": {"level": "quran", "claims": [{"text": "A company of jinn heard the Qur’an and believed", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 72:1-2"]}, {"text": "Among the jinn are righteous believers and unjust rebels, judged accordingly", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 72:11", "Qur’an 72:14-15"]}, {"text": "Jinn, like humans, were created to worship Allah", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 51:56"]}], "boundary": "The texts do not detail how jinn perform worship or give any census of their believers and rebels.", "note": ""}, "refs": ["Qur’an 72:1-15", "Qur’an 51:56"]}, {"id": "iblis-shaytan", "cat": "jinn", "title": "Iblis and Shaytan: the Name and the Role", "ar": "إِبْلِيسُ وَالشَّيْطَانُ", "hook": "One is a name, the other is a job description, and the Qur’an never confuses the two.", "body": ["Iblis is an individual: ’they prostrated, except Iblis. He was of the jinn, and he rebelled against the command of his Lord’ (18:50). One being, one refusal, one story. Shaytan is broader. Surah an-Nas seeks refuge from the whisperer ’from among the jinn and mankind’ (114:6), and Allah speaks of ’shayatin of mankind and jinn’ inspiring each other with adorned speech (6:112). Shaytan names a function, the rebel who whispers and misleads, and both species can fill it.", "Why does the distinction matter? Because 18:50 settles a real question: Iblis was of the jinn, not a fallen angel, since angels do not disobey (66:6). And because 114:6 protects us from a lazy assumption: that all our harm comes from the unseen. A human being can play the shaytan to another human. Keeping the name and the role separate keeps our theology clean and our vigilance honest, aimed at whispers wherever they come from, not at shadows."], "evidence": {"level": "quran", "claims": [{"text": "Iblis was of the jinn and refused the command to prostrate", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 18:50"]}, {"text": "Shaytan describes a role that rebellious jinn and humans can both play", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 114:6", "Qur’an 6:112"]}, {"text": "Angels do not disobey Allah, so Iblis was not a fallen angel", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 66:6"]}], "boundary": "The sources give Iblis no detailed biography beyond his refusal, his respite, and his declared enmity.", "note": ""}, "refs": ["Qur’an 18:50", "Qur’an 114:6", "Qur’an 6:112", "Qur’an 66:6"]}, {"id": "ifrit", "cat": "jinn", "title": "The Ifrit in Sulayman’s Court", "ar": "عِفْرِيتٌ مِّنَ الْجِنِّ", "hook": "The Qur’an mentions the ifrit exactly once, and what it actually says is more restrained than the stories.", "body": ["The scene is Sulayman’s council, seeking the throne of the queen of Saba: ’an ifrit from the jinn said: I will bring it to you before you rise from your place, and I am strong and trustworthy for it’ (27:39). From this single ayah we may honestly draw: ifrit describes a jinn of unusual strength, such a being could offer swift physical feats, and even this power sat under a prophet’s authority. Then a human with knowledge of the Book brought the throne faster (27:40), and the ifrit vanishes from revelation.", "Later literature built elaborate ranks of ifrit kings, armies and territories. None of that is in the ayah. Note also the ayah’s quiet correction of our instincts: the strongest jinn in the room was not the fastest way to Allah’s purpose; knowledge outran raw power. So we affirm what is written, a class of jinn marked by strength, and we file the rest under storytelling. In Bukhari there is also a report of the Prophet ﷺ restraining an ifrit who tried to interrupt his prayer, showing the word lived beyond this one court."], "evidence": {"level": "quran", "claims": [{"text": "An ifrit of the jinn offered to carry a throne across great distance, calling himself strong and trustworthy", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 27:39"]}, {"text": "The Prophet ﷺ spoke of seizing an ifrit that tried to interrupt his prayer", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 461"]}, {"text": "Elaborate ifrit hierarchies, kings and armies come from later storytelling, not revelation", "level": "editorial", "refs": []}], "boundary": "The sources establish a strong class of jinn and nothing about ifrit kingdoms, ranks, or named dynasties.", "note": ""}, "refs": ["Qur’an 27:39-40", "Bukhari 461"]}, {"id": "marid", "cat": "jinn", "title": "Marid: the Defiant Ones", "ar": "وَحِفْظًا مِّن كُلِّ شَيْطَانٍ مَّارِدٍ", "hook": "One Qur’anic word for rebellion produced a thousand tales; here is the part that is actually revelation.", "body": ["In Surah as-Saffat, Allah describes adorning the lower heaven with stars ’and a protection from every defiant shaytan’ (37:7). The word is marid, from a root carrying brazenness and persistence in rebellion. The same description recurs: people follow ’every rebellious shaytan’ (22:3), and idolaters call upon ’a defiant shaytan’ (4:117). So marid in revelation is an adjective of character, the hardened rebel among the shayatin, shut out from the highest assembly and repelled when it tries to listen (37:8-10).", "Later imagination turned the adjective into a species: towering marids in bottles, wish-granting captives, sea giants. Those images belong to One Thousand and One Nights and its cousins, not to the Qur’an or the Sunnah. The honest position holds the line in both directions. Revelation affirms defiant, hardened rebels among the shayatin, and says nothing of ranks, bottles, or wishes. When a book, film, or folk tale presents the marid as a catalogued creature with rules, it is quoting literature, and literature should be enjoyed, or declined, under its own name."], "evidence": {"level": "quran", "claims": [{"text": "The lower heaven is guarded from every marid, every defiant shaytan", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 37:7-10"]}, {"text": "Marid in the Qur’an is a description of brazen rebellion, used across several ayat", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 22:3", "Qur’an 4:117"]}, {"text": "The marid as a catalogued species with bottles, wishes and ranks is later folklore", "level": "editorial", "refs": []}], "boundary": "The sources describe a quality of rebellion, not a taxonomy of marid ranks, habitats, or powers.", "note": ""}, "refs": ["Qur’an 37:7-10", "Qur’an 22:3", "Qur’an 4:117"]}, {"id": "qareen", "cat": "jinn", "title": "The Qareen: Your Assigned Companion", "ar": "الْقَرِينُ", "hook": "Every person walks with an unseen companion, and the Prophet ﷺ did not exclude himself.", "body": ["The Prophet ﷺ said: ’there is no one among you but a companion from the jinn has been assigned to him.’ They asked: even you, Messenger of Allah? He said: ’even me, except that Allah helped me against him, fa-aslama, so he only commands me with good’ (Muslim 2814). Scholars transmit two readings of that word: fa-aslama, he submitted, or fa-aslamu, I am kept safe from him. Either way the meaning stands: the Prophet ﷺ was protected from his qareen’s harm, and we are not.", "The Qur’an lets a qareen speak at the end of the road. ’His companion will say: this is what is ready with me’ (50:23), and later, ’his companion will say: our Lord, I did not make him transgress, he was himself far astray’ (50:27). A lifetime of whispering, then a disowning. The practical lesson is neither paranoia nor denial. The qareen explains the pull we feel toward the low path, it does not excuse following it, and dhikr, wudu and the Qur’an starve its influence. Blame stays with the one who obeys the whisper."], "evidence": {"level": "sunnah", "claims": [{"text": "Every human being has an assigned companion from the jinn", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Muslim 2814"]}, {"text": "The Prophet’s ﷺ words about his own qareen carry two transmitted readings: it submitted, or he was protected from it", "level": "debated", "refs": ["Muslim 2814"]}, {"text": "A qareen will disown its human on the Day of Judgment", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 50:27"]}], "boundary": "The sources do not teach that the qareen controls a person or lifts responsibility for sin.", "note": "Many commentators read the qareen of 50:23 as the recording angel and the qareen of 50:27 as the shaytan companion."}, "refs": ["Muslim 2814", "Qur’an 50:23", "Qur’an 50:27"]}, {"id": "jinn-forms", "cat": "jinn", "title": "When Jinn Take Forms", "ar": "تَشَكُّلُ الْجِنِّ", "hook": "A snake in a Medinan house, a night visitor stealing dates: the sober reports are stranger than the folklore, and better sourced.", "body": ["Authentic reports do show jinn appearing in forms. In Sahih Muslim, the Prophet ﷺ explained that some house snakes in Medina may be Muslim jinn dwellers, to be given three days’ warning before harm is considered, after a young companion and a serpent died together (Muslim 2236). In Sahih al-Bukhari, a figure came by night to take from the zakat stores, bargained with Abu Hurairah, taught him Ayat al-Kursi, and the Prophet ﷺ confirmed: that was a shaytan (Bukhari 2311). These are careful, limited, documented cases.", "The sira adds that Shaytan appeared at Badr in the likeness of Suraqah ibn Malik, then fled, which commentators connect to ’and when Shaytan made their deeds fair-seeming to them’ (8:48); its chains are those of sira literature, so we label it reported, not certain. Beyond this the ground gets soft. Every village has tales of jinn as black dogs, brides, or old men on roads. Some may echo truth, most are unverifiable. Our rule: relate what the sahih relates, mark sira material as sira, and let folklore stand unendorsed."], "evidence": {"level": "mixed", "claims": [{"text": "Some house serpents in Medina may be jinn dwellers, and a three day notice was prescribed before harming them", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Muslim 2236"]}, {"text": "A shaytan came in perceivable form to Abu Hurairah on three nights and taught him Ayat al-Kursi", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 2311"]}, {"text": "Shaytan appeared at Badr in the likeness of Suraqah ibn Malik", "level": "debated", "refs": ["Qur’an 8:48", "Sirat Ibn Ishaq"]}, {"text": "Village shapeshifter tales of specific jinn forms are unverifiable folklore", "level": "editorial", "refs": []}], "boundary": "The sources do not teach that jinn can be identified on sight or that any particular animal is generally a jinn.", "note": ""}, "refs": ["Muslim 2236", "Bukhari 2311", "Qur’an 8:48", "Sirat Ibn Ishaq"]}, {"id": "jinn-protection", "cat": "jinn", "title": "The Lawful Shields", "ar": "آيَةُ الْكُرْسِيِّ", "hook": "The same hadith that proved a shaytan can visit also handed us the lock on the door.", "body": ["Protection in Islam is words, not objects. When the night visitor taught Abu Hurairah that reciting Ayat al-Kursi at night keeps a guardian from Allah with you until morning, the Prophet ﷺ said: he told you the truth, though he is a liar (Bukhari 2311). The Prophet ﷺ commanded closing doors, covering vessels and mentioning Allah’s name at night (Muslim 2012), and the sunnah gives the three Quls and the morning and evening adhkar as daily armour. Simple, repeatable, free.", "The forbidden route is equally clear. Whoever goes to a fortune teller and asks him about anything, prayer is not accepted from him for forty nights (Muslim 2230). Amulets and knotted charms fall under the Prophet’s ﷺ severe warnings against tamaim (Musnad Ahmad). The logic is tawhid: protection is sought from Allah by His words, never purchased from intermediaries who trade on fear. Before sleep the Prophet ﷺ would recite the three Quls into his palms and wipe over his body (Bukhari 5017). That, not a talisman, is the believer’s nightly ritual."], "evidence": {"level": "sunnah", "claims": [{"text": "Reciting Ayat al-Kursi at night brings a guardian from Allah until morning", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 2311"]}, {"text": "Doors are closed, vessels covered and Allah’s name mentioned at night as protection", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Muslim 2012"]}, {"text": "Asking a fortune teller strips forty nights of prayer of acceptance", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Muslim 2230"]}, {"text": "Wearing amulets for protection is condemned in the hadith", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Musnad Ahmad"]}], "boundary": "The sources promise protection through remembrance, not immunity from every hardship, and prescribe no objects to wear.", "note": ""}, "refs": ["Bukhari 2311", "Muslim 2012", "Muslim 2230", "Bukhari 5017", "Musnad Ahmad"]}, {"id": "ghoul", "cat": "myth", "title": "The Ghoul: a Belief Corrected", "ar": "لَا غُولَ", "hook": "Pre-Islamic Arabia populated the night desert with a monster, and the Prophet ﷺ dismissed it in two words.", "body": ["The Arabs before Islam told of the ghul, a desert creature that shifted shapes, lit false fires and led travellers off the road to devour them. Whole journeys were narrated around escaping it. Then came a short, sweeping correction: ’la adwa wa la tiyarata wa la ghula’, no contagion without decree, no evil omens, and no ghoul (Sahih Muslim, the hadith family around Muslim 2222). A superstition that had organised the night fears of a whole people was cancelled in a clause.", "What exactly was negated? Commentators explain la ghula as striking the pagan claim itself: an independent night species with its own power to misguide and kill. Several of them allowed that rebellious jinn may still play tricks on travellers, while insisting the ghoul of Arab lore, a creature with a fixed form and its own dominion over the desert, is denied. The correction is therefore precise, not sweeping. It removes a superstition. It does not touch the jinn, who are established by the Qur’an with separate evidence, and it leaves travellers with dhikr rather than dread."], "evidence": {"level": "corrected", "claims": [{"text": "Pre-Islamic Arabs held the ghoul to be an independent desert creature that misled and devoured travellers", "level": "editorial", "refs": ["Muslim 2222"]}, {"text": "The Prophet ﷺ declared la ghula, there is no ghoul", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Muslim 2222"]}, {"text": "The negation removes the pagan species claim while the jinn remain separately established", "level": "editorial", "refs": ["Qur’an 72:1"]}], "boundary": "This correction does not deny the jinn, whose reality is established separately in the Qur’an.", "note": "Level marked corrected because revelation here removes a belief rather than adds one."}, "refs": ["Muslim 2222", "Sahih Muslim"]}, {"id": "dragons", "cat": "myth", "title": "Dragons: What the Texts Actually Say", "ar": "التِّنِّينُ", "hook": "Search the Qur’an and the authentic Sunnah for a dragon and you will return with serpents, and honesty.", "body": ["Revelation knows serpents well. Musa’s staff became a hayyah, a serpent, ’a manifest thubaan’ (20:20, 7:107), and a punishing serpent called shujaa aqra appears in the hadith of the withheld zakat (Bukhari 1403). What revelation never establishes is a dragon: a winged, fire-breathing species of independent existence. The word tinnin, Arabic for dragon, appears in later cosmography, story collections and some tafsir marginalia, not in the Qur’an and not in any authentic hadith we can point to.", "So what should a Muslim say about dragons? Not established. That is different from proven impossible, and the difference is the whole discipline of this room. Allah’s creation is vaster than our inventories, ’and He creates what you do not know’ (16:8), so we do not legislate impossibility. But belief follows evidence, and no evidence obliges belief in dragons. They remain what they are: a motif of human storytelling from China to Wales, enjoyed as literature, carrying no weight in aqidah, and requiring no fatwa to appreciate on a bookshelf."], "evidence": {"level": "debated", "claims": [{"text": "Musa’s staff truly became a serpent by Allah’s power", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 20:20", "Qur’an 7:107"]}, {"text": "A serpent of punishment, shujaa aqra, is described for the withholder of zakat", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 1403"]}, {"text": "No independent dragon species is established by the Qur’an or authentic Sunnah; tinnin belongs to later literature", "level": "editorial", "refs": []}, {"text": "Allah creates what we do not know, so impossibility is not declared either", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 16:8"]}], "boundary": "The sources establish neither the existence nor the impossibility of dragons; silence is the finding.", "note": "Level marked debated to reflect a question the texts leave open rather than settle."}, "refs": ["Qur’an 20:20", "Qur’an 7:107", "Qur’an 16:8", "Bukhari 1403"]}, {"id": "evil-eye", "cat": "myth", "title": "The Evil Eye: Real, and Leashed", "ar": "الْعَيْنُ حَقٌّ", "hook": "The Prophet ﷺ affirmed the eye is real, then Islam immediately regulated everything about how we respond to it.", "body": ["’Al-aynu haqq’, the evil eye is real (Bukhari 5740, Muslim 2188). The Muslim narration adds a striking clause: if anything were to outrun the decree, the eye would have, meaning even this force operates strictly inside Allah’s qadar. Nothing strikes independently. The sunnah then arms both sides of the exchange: the admirer is taught to invoke barakah on what pleases him rather than project envy (Muwatta Malik), and the affected seek ruqyah, for the Prophet ﷺ permitted and commanded ruqyah for the eye (Bukhari 5738).", "Now the fences. The reality of the eye never licenses an accusation culture: naming an aunt as the cause of a fever, splitting families over suspicions, hiring practitioners to identify culprits. The Qur’an’s remedy for envy is refuge in Allah ’from the evil of an envier when he envies’ (113:5), not counter-attack. And affirming the unseen cause never cancels the seen one: medicine is sought, doctors are visited, and ruqyah accompanies treatment rather than replacing it. The eye is real; adab around it is the actual test."], "evidence": {"level": "sunnah", "claims": [{"text": "The evil eye is real", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 5740", "Muslim 2188"]}, {"text": "The eye operates only within Allah’s decree", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Muslim 2188"]}, {"text": "Ruqyah is prescribed for the one affected by the eye", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 5738"]}, {"text": "Accusing specific people of casting the eye has no basis in the sunnah and breeds injustice", "level": "editorial", "refs": ["Qur’an 113:5"]}], "boundary": "The sources give no method to identify a culprit by suspicion and never present ruqyah as a substitute for medical care.", "note": ""}, "refs": ["Bukhari 5740", "Muslim 2188", "Bukhari 5738", "Qur’an 113:5", "Muwatta Malik"]}, {"id": "shooting-stars", "cat": "mysteries", "title": "Shooting Stars and the Eavesdroppers", "ar": "شِهَابٌ ثَاقِبٌ", "hook": "The jinn themselves report a change in the sky’s security, and the Qur’an preserves their words.", "body": ["In Surah al-Jinn, the jinn speak: ’we touched the heaven and found it filled with severe guards and burning flames. We used to sit there on seats to listen, but whoever listens now finds a flame lying in wait for him’ (72:8-9). Other ayat confirm the picture: the lower heaven adorned and guarded from every rebellious shaytan (37:6-8), and any who steals a hearing is pursued by ’a clear burning flame’ (15:17-18). The unseen assembly is protected; stolen fragments reach soothsayers mixed with a hundred lies.", "Here a boundary must be drawn with care. These ayat describe the policing of the unseen, the assembly no telescope observes. They are not a prohibition of astronomy, and they do not ask us to deny what observatories document: meteors are physical objects, rock and metal igniting in the atmosphere, predictable in showers like the Perseids. The Qur’an speaks of shuhub in their unseen function; science describes their material behaviour. Two planes, both real, neither cancelling the other, and a Muslim may study meteor physics with a clear conscience."], "evidence": {"level": "quran", "claims": [{"text": "The heaven is guarded and eavesdropping shayatin are repelled by burning flames", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 72:8-9", "Qur’an 15:17-18", "Qur’an 37:6-10"]}, {"text": "Fragments stolen by eavesdroppers reach soothsayers mixed with many lies", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Sahih al-Bukhari"]}, {"text": "These ayat do not forbid astronomy or contradict the physical study of meteors", "level": "editorial", "refs": []}], "boundary": "The ayat describe the protection of the unseen assembly and do not claim that every observed meteor is a strike against a jinn.", "note": ""}, "refs": ["Qur’an 72:8-9", "Qur’an 15:17-18", "Qur’an 37:6-10", "Sahih al-Bukhari"]}, {"id": "sihr", "cat": "mysteries", "title": "Sihr: Real, Forbidden, Leashed", "ar": "السِّحْرُ", "hook": "The Qur’an confirms magic exists in the same breath that it strips magic of independent power.", "body": ["The key ayah recounts what the shayatin taught at Babil, then inserts the leash: ’and they harm none through it except by the permission of Allah’ (2:102). Sihr is real, learnable, and utterly dependent; it wounds only where the decree allows. The same ayah rules on its practitioners: they have ’no share in the akhirah’, and the Prophet ﷺ counted sihr among the seven destructive sins (Bukhari 2766). Real, therefore taken seriously; forbidden, therefore never learned, purchased, or hired, even against an enemy.", "The Prophet ﷺ himself was touched by it: Labid ibn al-Asam worked sihr on him, he was affected for a period, Allah informed him of the place, and he said: as for me, Allah has healed me, and I feared to stir evil among the people (Bukhari 5763). Some reports place the revelation of the two Quls, al-Falaq and an-Nas, in that context; the connection is transmitted in tafsir and sira literature rather than the two Sahihs, so we mark it reported. The treatment door is open: ruqyah with Qur’an and authentic adhkar. The counter-sorcerer’s door is shut; sihr is not healed by more sihr."], "evidence": {"level": "mixed", "claims": [{"text": "Sihr harms no one except by the permission of Allah", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 2:102"]}, {"text": "Practicing sihr is forbidden and counted among the destructive sins", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 2:102", "Bukhari 2766"]}, {"text": "The Prophet ﷺ was affected by sihr for a period and Allah healed him", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 5763"]}, {"text": "The two Quls were revealed in connection with that incident", "level": "debated", "refs": ["al-Wahidi, Asbab an-Nuzul"]}], "boundary": "The sources permit treating sihr with ruqyah and give no permission to practice it, hire it, or counter it with sorcery.", "note": "Scholars stress the affliction touched outward matters and never the revelation the Prophet ﷺ conveyed."}, "refs": ["Qur’an 2:102", "Bukhari 5763", "Bukhari 2766", "al-Wahidi, Asbab an-Nuzul"]}, {"id": "dreams", "cat": "mysteries", "title": "Three Kinds of Dreams", "ar": "الرُّؤْيَا", "hook": "Not every dream is a message; the Prophet ﷺ gave us a filing system of three drawers.", "body": ["The Prophet ﷺ said dreams are of three kinds: the true dream, glad tidings from Allah; the dream of sorrow, from Shaytan; and the dream rising from what a person tells himself during the day (Muslim 2263). This triage is mercy. It frees us from treating every night image as an omen, while keeping the door open, for the righteous dream was described as a portion of prophethood’s light. Most of what we see at night is the third drawer: the mind replaying its own traffic.", "The adab follows the sorting. A good dream: praise Allah and share it only with those you love and the wise, who will not envy or mangle it. A disliked dream: spit drily to the left, seek refuge in Allah from its evil, change your side, and tell no one, for it will not harm you (Sahih al-Bukhari, Sahih Muslim). And do not chase an interpretation for every dream or hand your nights to online interpreters; Yusuf’s gift was prophetic, ours is mostly guesswork, and a believer’s mornings belong to the day’s work, not the night’s riddles."], "evidence": {"level": "sunnah", "claims": [{"text": "Dreams are of three kinds: from Allah, from Shaytan, and from the self", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Muslim 2263"]}, {"text": "Good dreams are shared with those one loves; after a bad dream one seeks refuge, spits drily left, and tells no one, and it will not harm", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Muslim 2263", "Sahih al-Bukhari"]}, {"text": "The true dream is described as a part of prophethood", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Sahih al-Bukhari", "Sahih Muslim"]}, {"text": "Chasing an interpretation for every dream is discouraged practice, not a religious duty", "level": "editorial", "refs": []}], "boundary": "The sources establish the categories and the etiquette, not a dictionary fixing one meaning per symbol for every dreamer.", "note": ""}, "refs": ["Muslim 2263", "Sahih al-Bukhari", "Sahih Muslim"]}, {"id": "sleep-souls", "cat": "mysteries", "title": "Sleep and the Returning Soul", "ar": "اللَّهُ يَتَوَفَّى الْأَنفُسَ حِينَ مَوْتِهَا", "hook": "Every night you hand something back to its Owner, and every morning it is either returned or kept.", "body": ["’Allah takes the souls at the time of their death, and those that have not died, in their sleep. He keeps those against which He has decreed death and releases the others until an appointed term. Surely in that are signs for a people who reflect’ (39:42). The ayah names sleep a wafah, a taking, softer than death’s but the same verb. The Prophet ﷺ voiced it in the night du’a: in Your name I die and I live, and on waking: praise to Allah who gave us life after He caused us to die, and to Him is the resurrection (Bukhari 6312).", "So a third of every life is spent rehearsing the exchange at its end: surrender, custody, return. Whoever sleeps has practised dying; whoever wakes has been granted a small resurrection and should treat the day as issued, not owed. That is the ayah’s stated purpose, signs for people who reflect. What the ayah is not: a physiology textbook. It does not map REM stages, licence claims that the sleeping body is empty, or ground sellers of unseen sleep cures. The soul’s custody is Allah’s affair; the sleep clinic remains a perfectly halal address."], "evidence": {"level": "quran", "claims": [{"text": "Allah takes souls at death and in sleep, keeping some and returning others until an appointed term", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur’an 39:42"]}, {"text": "The Prophet ﷺ framed sleeping and waking as dying and being given life", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 6312"]}, {"text": "The ayah calls to reflection on death and resurrection, not to pseudo-medical claims about sleep", "level": "editorial", "refs": []}], "boundary": "The ayah teaches Allah’s custody of souls and supplies no medical claims about sleep states.", "note": ""}, "refs": ["Qur’an 39:42", "Bukhari 6312"]}, {"id": "ad", "cat": "peoples", "title": "The People of ’Ad", "ar": "قَوْمُ عَادٍ", "hook": "A people amplified in strength asked who could be mightier, and the wind answered for seven nights and eight days.", "body": ["After the people of Nuh had passed, the Qur’an says, Allah made ’Ad successors in the earth and increased them abundantly in stature and strength. To them He sent their brother Hud, who asked nothing but that they worship Allah alone and remember the favor that had raised them. Their answer was the boast the Qur’an preserves, who is mightier than us in strength, and its reply is immediate: the One who created them is mightier. They clung to what their fathers served, and the argument closed with a promise of punishment they had dared their prophet to hasten.", "The end came by wind, a barren, screaming wind that Allah imposed on them seven nights and eight days without pause. Surah al-Haqqah lets us see the morning after: a people thrown down as if they were hollow trunks of palm trees, and asks whether any remnant of them can be found. Surah al-Fajr names their glory, Iram of the pillars, the like of which had not been created in the lands, and al-Ahqaf names their home among the wind curved dunes. Strength, the passage teaches, is an argument for the Maker, never against Him.", "How tall were they? The Qur’an says only that their creation was amplified, and it lets that suffice. Later reports circulated exact figures in cubits, sometimes vast ones, yet these travel through chains and lore whose soundness is debated, not through the Book or the established Sunnah, and this room does not state them as fact. What is established is the lesson ’Ad were built to carry: no measure of body or building has ever held back the command of Allah."], "evidence": {"level": "mixed", "claims": [{"text": "Allah made ’Ad successors after the people of Nuh and increased them abundantly in stature and strength", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur'an 7:69", "Qur'an 41:15"]}, {"text": "They were destroyed by a furious, barren wind imposed on them seven nights and eight days, leaving them like hollow trunks of palm trees", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur'an 69:6-8", "Qur'an 41:16"]}, {"text": "Iram of the pillars, the like of which had not been created in the lands, names their glory", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur'an 89:6-8"]}, {"text": "Exact heights in cubits for ’Ad circulate only in later reports and are not stated here as fact", "level": "debated", "refs": ["Tafsir literature"]}], "boundary": "The sources give no measurements for ’Ad, no location for Iram beyond the homeland at al-Ahqaf, and no surviving ruin identified with them; matching Iram to any excavated site is not established.", "note": "Qur’an 46:21 places Hud’s warning at al-Ahqaf, the region of the dunes; that is as far as the map goes."}, "refs": ["Qur'an 7:65-72", "Qur'an 41:15-16", "Qur'an 69:6-8", "Qur'an 89:6-8", "Qur'an 46:21", "Qur'an 54:18-21"]}, {"id": "thamud", "cat": "peoples", "title": "Thamud and the She-Camel", "ar": "ثَمُودُ", "hook": "To a people who carved mountains into homes, Allah sent a she-camel as a test, and one cry ended the trial.", "body": ["Thamud rose after ’Ad, settled where plains gave them palaces and mountains gave them carved houses, and to them Allah sent their brother Salih. When they demanded proof, the proof came walking: the she-camel of Allah, a clear sign from their Lord. The command was gentle and exact, let her eat in Allah’s earth, the water is shared between her and you, and do not touch her with harm. Surah ash-Shams tells the ending in five verses: the most wretched of them rose, the she-camel was hamstrung, and their Lord crushed them for their sin and made them level.", "Surah al-Hijr calls them the companions of al-Hijr, who denied the messengers and carved secure homes from the mountains, till the cry seized them at morning and nothing they had earned availed them. Surah Hud adds the mercy inside the warning: after the she-camel was hamstrung they were granted three days in their homes, a promise not to be belied. Security cut from rock, the passage teaches, is still only rock; the only safety that morning belonged to Salih and those who believed with him.", "Centuries later, on the road to Tabuk, the Prophet ﷺ passed the dwellings at al-Hijr and told his companions not to enter upon the punished people except weeping, lest what struck them strike those who pass. The place is identified with the site now called Mada’in Salih in northwest Arabia, an editorial note from geography rather than revelation, and archaeologists date its standing rock cut facades to the later Nabataean period. The stone remembers builders; the Qur’an remembers why building was not enough."], "evidence": {"level": "quran", "claims": [{"text": "The she-camel was sent as a clear sign and a trial, the water divided between her and them, and they hamstrung her", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur'an 7:73", "Qur'an 54:27-28", "Qur'an 91:13-14"]}, {"text": "They carved secure homes from the mountains, and the cry seized them at morning after three days of respite", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur'an 15:80-84", "Qur'an 11:65-67"]}, {"text": "Passing al-Hijr, the Prophet ﷺ told his companions not to enter upon the punished people except weeping", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Muslim 2980"]}, {"text": "Mada’in Salih in northwest Arabia is identified with al-Hijr, while its standing rock cut facades are dated by archaeologists to the later Nabataean period, so no visible structure is attributed to Thamud", "level": "editorial", "refs": []}], "boundary": "The sources do not date Thamud, do not describe the she-camel beyond her being a sign with a right to water, and do not tie any particular surviving facade to them.", "note": "The punishment is called a quake in 7:78 and a cry in 11:67; mufassirin read these as one seizure described from two sides."}, "refs": ["Qur'an 7:73-79", "Qur'an 11:61-68", "Qur'an 91:11-15", "Qur'an 15:80-84", "Qur'an 54:23-31", "Muslim 2980"]}, {"id": "yajuj-majuj", "cat": "peoples", "title": "Yajuj and Majuj", "ar": "يَأْجُوجُ وَمَأْجُوجُ", "hook": "Behind a wall of iron and molten copper waits a people whose release is written among the last promises.", "body": ["In Surah al-Kahf, Dhul-Qarnayn reaches a people between two mountain walls who can barely understand speech, and they plead for help against Yajuj and Majuj, corrupters in the land. He refuses their payment, asks instead for strength of hands, and builds: blocks of iron filling the gap, bellows worked until the metal glows like fire, molten copper poured over the whole. The Qur’an records the result and its limit in one breath, they could not climb over it and they could not pierce it, then his own words: this is a mercy from my Lord, and when my Lord’s promise comes, He will level it.", "Their release belongs to the grammar of the end. Surah al-Anbiya binds it to the approach of the true promise: when Yajuj and Majuj are opened and they slide down from every height, the eyes of the disbelievers will stare. In Muslim 2937 they emerge after Isa ﷺ has descended and the Dajjal is gone, a host no one has power to fight, ended not by armies but by Allah’s command. And when Bukhari 3348 describes the delegation of the Fire, nine hundred ninety nine of every thousand, the Prophet ﷺ comforted his companions that the count is filled from Yajuj and Majuj, one man of you beside a thousand of them.", "A narration in Tirmidhi describes them digging at the barrier each day until they nearly see sunlight, told to return tomorrow, and finding it restored, until the day their leader adds, if Allah wills, and it stands as they left it, and they break out. Scholars have discussed this report’s grading and its fit with the verse that they could not pierce the barrier, so this room cites it as a report, not a certainty. Where the barrier stands, what they look like, and how many they are, the sources leave unsaid, and we leave it unsaid with them."], "evidence": {"level": "quran", "claims": [{"text": "Dhul-Qarnayn built a barrier of iron and molten copper that Yajuj and Majuj could neither scale nor pierce, and when Allah’s promise comes He will level it", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur'an 18:94-98"]}, {"text": "Their opening, hastening down from every height, is bound to the drawing near of the true promise", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur'an 21:96-97"]}, {"text": "They emerge after the descent of Isa ﷺ and are ended by Allah’s command, not by human strength", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Muslim 2937"]}, {"text": "In the hadith of the delegation of the Fire, nine hundred and ninety nine of every thousand, the Prophet ﷺ comforted his companions that the number is filled from Yajuj and Majuj, one man of you beside a thousand of them", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 3348"]}], "boundary": "The sources assign them no faces, no sizes, and no census, and they fix no map reference; identifying the barrier with any known wall, or them with any modern nation, is not established.", "note": "The report of daily digging at the barrier is cited from Tirmidhi as a report; its grading is discussed among hadith scholars, and the Qur’an’s wording that they could not pierce it stands as revealed."}, "refs": ["Qur'an 18:93-99", "Qur'an 21:96-97", "Muslim 2937", "Bukhari 3348", "Tirmidhi"]}, {"id": "qawm-nuh", "cat": "peoples", "title": "The People of Nuh and the Long Patience", "ar": "قَوْمُ نُوحٍ", "hook": "Nine hundred and fifty years of calling ended with a door that closed as the sky’s gates opened.", "body": ["The Qur’an gives the span itself: Nuh remained among his people a thousand years less fifty, calling them to their Lord. Surah Nuh preserves the shape of that patience, I called them night and day, openly and in secret; they pressed their fingers into their ears and pulled their garments over themselves and grew arrogant. He promised them what repentance buys, forgiveness, rain in abundance, wealth and sons, gardens and rivers. Generations were born, heard the same truthful voice, and taught their children to refuse it, clinging to Wadd and Suwa and the idols the Qur’an names.", "Surah al-Qamar carries the ending in a rush of verses: they called Our servant a liar and said, madman, and he was rebuked, so he called his Lord, I am overcome, so give victory. Then the gates of the sky were opened with pouring water and the earth burst with springs, and the waters met by a decree already measured. Nuh was carried on a thing of planks and nails, running under Our eyes, a recompense for the one denied. Of his people the Qur’an says they were drowned for their sins and made to enter a Fire; it was left as a sign, the surah says, so is there any who will remember?", "How far did the water reach? The Qur’an fixes the moral scope with perfect clarity, the people who denied Nuh were destroyed, none of the believers were lost, and Nuh prayed that no dweller from the disbelievers be left upon the earth. What it does not give is a map. Whether the flood covered the whole planet or the whole of Nuh’s people and their lands is a question scholars have discussed, and this entry marks it debated rather than deciding it. The certainty is the lesson: denial drowned, and gratitude sailed."], "evidence": {"level": "quran", "claims": [{"text": "Nuh remained among his people a thousand years less fifty, and the flood seized them while they were wrongdoers", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur'an 29:14"]}, {"text": "He called them night and day, openly and in secret, and they pressed their fingers into their ears and persisted in arrogance", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur'an 71:5-9"]}, {"text": "The sky’s gates were opened with pouring water, the earth burst with springs, and Nuh was carried on a vessel of planks and nails, left as a sign", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur'an 54:11-15"]}, {"text": "Whether the flood covered the entire earth or encompassed the people of Nuh and their lands is discussed among scholars; the Qur’an states who was destroyed, not the flood’s map", "level": "debated", "refs": ["Qur'an 71:26", "Tafsir literature"]}], "boundary": "The sources do not give the flood’s geography or the ark’s dimensions, and beyond the name al-Judi they do not locate its resting place; counts of those aboard come from reports, not the Qur’an.", "note": "Qur’an 11:40 says only that those who believed with him were few."}, "refs": ["Qur'an 29:14-15", "Qur'an 71:5-27", "Qur'an 54:9-16", "Qur'an 11:36-48"]}, {"id": "dabbat-alard", "cat": "signs", "title": "Dabbat al-Ard, the Beast of the Earth", "ar": "دَابَّةُ الْأَرْضِ", "hook": "Of all the signs, this one is a single verse: the earth gives up a creature, and it speaks.", "body": ["Surah an-Naml holds the entire Qur’anic knowledge of this sign in one verse. When the word falls due upon people, Allah says, We shall bring out for them a creature from the earth that will speak to them, because people were not certain of Our signs. Every element is weighed: it comes when the sentence has already fallen, it is brought out, not born of human plans, it is from the earth itself, and its work is speech, an address to a humanity that ran out of certainty. The verse gives no shape, no size, no species, and no place.", "The Sunnah confirms its rank. In Muslim 2901 the Beast stands among the ten great signs the Hour will not arrive without, and in Muslim 158 it is named with the Dajjal and the sun rising from its west among signs after which faith no longer benefits a soul that had not believed before. Everything else, its form, its tools, the reports that it marks the faces of believers and disbelievers, comes through narrations whose grading scholars dispute, and through tafsir traditions of uneven strength. This room records that material as claimed, not as known.", "A rule of this room follows from that honesty: the Beast is never illustrated here, and no animal form is assigned to it. Where later pages painted composite creatures, the Qur’an chose restraint, and restraint is part of the sign’s power. What is certain is the order and the meaning: it comes when the word has already fallen due, and once it speaks, the age of arguing about the unseen is over."], "evidence": {"level": "mixed", "claims": [{"text": "When the word falls due upon people, a creature from the earth is brought out that speaks to them, because people were not certain of Allah’s signs", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur'an 27:82"]}, {"text": "The Beast is one of the ten great signs before the Hour", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Muslim 2901"]}, {"text": "It is named with the Dajjal and the western sunrise among signs after which faith no longer benefits one who had not believed before", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Muslim 158"]}, {"text": "Its form, size, place of emergence, and the marking of faces come from reports and tafsir traditions whose grading is disputed", "level": "debated", "refs": ["Tafsir literature"]}], "boundary": "The sources do not establish what the Beast looks like, where it emerges, or its species; reports naming particular features or places are disputed and are not stated as fact.", "note": "Rule of this room: no illustration or animal form is ever assigned to the Beast, because the Qur’an assigns none."}, "refs": ["Qur'an 27:82", "Muslim 2901", "Muslim 158"]}, {"id": "dukhan", "cat": "signs", "title": "The Smoke", "ar": "الدُّخَانُ", "hook": "Between a famine remembered and a sign still awaited stands one verse of smoke that covers the people.", "body": ["So watch, Surah ad-Dukhan commands, for a day when the sky brings a visible smoke that envelops the people; this is a painful punishment. The next verse records the plea it will draw out, our Lord, remove the punishment from us, we are believers, and the answer asks how they will remember now, when a clear Messenger already came and was turned away. The scene is brief and vivid: a sky that testifies, a people wrapped in what they cannot argue with, and a plea arriving late.", "Here the mufassirin genuinely part ways, and this room shows both paths. Ibn Mas’ud, in narrations recorded by Bukhari, held that the smoke had already passed: when Quraysh refused, the Prophet ﷺ prayed against them with years like the years of Yusuf, and hunger thinned the world until a man looked at the sky and saw something like smoke; among five signs he counted as passed was the smoke. Other companions and early scholars held that the smoke is still ahead, a real covering of the sky among the last signs, and the Prophet’s ﷺ list of ten signs before the Hour in Muslim 2901 includes the Smoke.", "Some scholars reconcile the two, a smoke that touched Quraysh as a foretaste and a greater smoke to come; others keep to one side. This entry does not force the verdict. What can be said with a clear conscience is this: the verse is certain, the command to watch is certain, the Smoke appears in the Prophet’s ﷺ list of great signs, and the exact placement between past and future is a matter the earliest readers of the verse already discussed."], "evidence": {"level": "mixed", "claims": [{"text": "The Qur’an commands watching for a day when the sky brings a visible smoke that covers the people, called a painful punishment", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur'an 44:10-11"]}, {"text": "The Smoke is named in the ten great signs before the Hour", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Muslim 2901"]}, {"text": "Whether the verse was fulfilled in the famine that pressed Quraysh, as Ibn Mas’ud explained, or awaits fulfillment as a future sign, or both, is a standing discussion among the mufassirin", "level": "debated", "refs": ["Bukhari", "Tafsir literature"]}], "boundary": "The sources do not fix the Smoke’s timing, duration, or cause, and identifying it with any modern haze, eruption, or event is not established.", "note": "Read 44:10-16 whole: the plea to lift the punishment, the reminder of a clear Messenger turned away, and the day of the greatest seizing."}, "refs": ["Qur'an 44:10-16", "Muslim 2901", "Bukhari"]}, {"id": "sun-west", "cat": "signs", "title": "The Sun Rising from Its Setting Place", "ar": "طُلُوعُ الشَّمْسِ مِنْ مَغْرِبِهَا", "hook": "One morning the sun will rise where it has always set, and the longest open door in creation will close.", "body": ["The Prophet ﷺ said that the Hour will not stand until the sun rises from its setting place; when it rises and the people see it, all of them will believe, and that is the moment when faith benefits no soul that had not believed before or earned good in its faith. Bukhari places the report under the verse of al-An’am it echoes, the day some of your Lord’s signs come, and the narration itself recites it. Nothing in creation’s routine is more trusted than the sun’s path; the sign is precisely the breaking of the world’s most reliable habit.", "Why this sign closes the door, the Sunnah explains elsewhere: Allah extends His hand by night to accept the repentance of the day’s sinner, and extends His hand by day to accept the repentance of the night’s sinner, until the sun rises from its west. Repentance lives while the world’s order holds; a sunrise from the west announces that the order is being folded away. Muslim 2901 counts this among the ten great signs, and Muslim 158 groups it with the Dajjal and the Beast as the signs after which belief no longer avails one who had not believed."], "evidence": {"level": "sunnah", "claims": [{"text": "The Hour will not stand until the sun rises from its setting place; when the people see it, all will believe, at the moment faith no longer benefits one who had not believed before", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 4636", "Muslim 157"]}, {"text": "Allah extends His hand by night and by day to accept repentance until the sun rises from its west", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Muslim"]}, {"text": "The narration itself joins this sign to the verse, the day some of your Lord’s signs come, no soul will benefit from its faith", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 4636", "Qur'an 6:158"]}], "boundary": "The sources do not give a date, a mechanism, or a picture of that morning beyond the rising itself; astronomical speculation is not part of the evidence.", "note": ""}, "refs": ["Bukhari 4636", "Muslim 157", "Qur'an 6:158", "Muslim 2901"]}, {"id": "great-signs", "cat": "signs", "title": "The Ten Great Signs in One Map", "ar": "أَشْرَاطُ السَّاعَةِ الْكُبْرَى", "hook": "Ten signs, one report: the Hour does not arrive until the world has seen them all.", "body": ["The companions were discussing the Hour when the Prophet ﷺ came upon them and asked what they were saying. Then he gave them the list that this whole room keeps returning to: it will not stand until you see ten signs, the Smoke, the Dajjal, the Beast, the rising of the sun from its setting place, the descent of Isa son of Maryam ﷺ, Yajuj and Majuj, and three sinkings of the earth, one in the east, one in the west, one in the Peninsula of the Arabs, and the last of that a fire emerging from Yemen, driving the people to their place of gathering.", "Honesty about this list includes honesty about its order. The transmissions of the same hadith arrange the ten differently, and the report does not number a sequence. What can be ordered is what other evidence orders: the Dajjal, then the descent of Isa ﷺ who ends him, then the release of Yajuj and Majuj, as the account of Muslim 2937 runs; and a narration in Muslim makes the western sunrise, with the Beast at its heels, first among the signs in one respect, a firstness scholars discuss. So this tile is a map, not a timetable: ten certainties, their full sequence left folded.", "Four of the ten have their own entries in this room, the Smoke, the Beast, the western sunrise, and Yajuj and Majuj; the Dajjal and the descent of Isa ﷺ carry entire literatures of their own. The three sinkings and the final fire stand in the hadith with almost no added description, and that is how they stand here: named, awaited, and not embroidered."], "evidence": {"level": "sunnah", "claims": [{"text": "The Hour will not stand until ten signs are seen: the Smoke, the Dajjal, the Beast, the sun rising from its setting place, the descent of Isa son of Maryam ﷺ, Yajuj and Majuj, three sinkings of the earth, and a fire from Yemen driving people to their gathering", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Muslim 2901"]}, {"text": "Within the ten, the Dajjal, then the descent of Isa ﷺ who ends him, then the release of Yajuj and Majuj follow one another", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Muslim 2937"]}, {"text": "One narration makes the western sunrise, with the Beast close on its heels, first among the signs in one respect, a firstness scholars discuss", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Muslim"]}, {"text": "The full order of the ten is not decisively fixed; transmissions of the hadith vary in arrangement, and this room presents the list as a map, not a timetable", "level": "editorial", "refs": ["Muslim 2901"]}], "boundary": "The sources provide no dates and no complete sequence for the ten, and they do not identify any present event with any sign; only a few relative orders are established.", "note": "A variant wording of the hadith has the fire beginning from the depth of Aden."}, "refs": ["Muslim 2901", "Muslim 2937"]}, {"id": "zakat-serpent", "cat": "signs", "title": "The Serpent of Hoarded Wealth", "ar": "الشُّجَاعُ الْأَقْرَعُ", "hook": "Wealth that refused its duty returns on the Day as a serpent that says, I am your treasure.", "body": ["The Prophet ﷺ said: whoever Allah gives wealth and he does not pay its zakat, his wealth will be made to appear for him on the Day of Resurrection as a bald headed serpent with two dark marks, it will be wrapped around him like a collar, it will seize him by the corners of his mouth, and it will say, I am your wealth, I am your treasure. Then he recited the verse of Al Imran: let them not think that what they withheld of Allah’s bounty is good for them; they will be collared with what they withheld on the Day of Resurrection.", "The Qur’an’s own imagery is fire rather than serpent: those who hoard gold and silver and do not spend it in the way of Allah are promised a Day when it is heated in the fire of Jahannam and their foreheads, sides, and backs are branded with it, this is what you hoarded for yourselves, so taste what you used to hoard. In Muslim 987 the hoard becomes plates of fire pressed to the owner through a Day fifty thousand years long, until judgment is passed. The wealth is not lost; it is returned, transformed into testimony.", "Read honestly, this is a scene of the Reckoning, not a monster of the world. The hadith’s own verb says the wealth is made to appear so for its keeper on that Day; nothing in the sources sets a serpent loose in the earth before it. The category matters: the terror here is not zoological but moral, the reunion of a person with what he was given and what he withheld. The door out is equally plain, and it is open now: give the wealth its right."], "evidence": {"level": "sunnah", "claims": [{"text": "Wealth whose zakat was withheld is made to appear on the Day of Resurrection as a bald headed serpent with two dark marks that collars its keeper, seizes his jaws, and says, I am your wealth, I am your treasure", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 1403"]}, {"text": "Those who withhold what Allah gave them of His bounty will be collared with it on the Day of Resurrection", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur'an 3:180"]}, {"text": "Hoarded gold and silver will be heated in the fire of Jahannam and used to brand foreheads, sides, and backs", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur'an 9:34-35"]}, {"text": "This is reckoning imagery for the one who refused zakat, wealth given a form on that Day, not a creature said to roam the earth now", "level": "editorial", "refs": ["Bukhari 1403"]}], "boundary": "The sources do not describe a serpent alive in the world today; the scene belongs to the Day of Resurrection and to the one who withheld the right of his wealth.", "note": "The hadith’s wording, his wealth will be made to appear for him, fixes this as a scene of the Reckoning, and the entry is categorized accordingly."}, "refs": ["Bukhari 1403", "Muslim 987", "Qur'an 3:180", "Qur'an 9:34-35"]}], "nj": {"intro": {"title": "The Night Journey", "ar": "الْإِسْرَاء وَالْمِعْرَاج", "lead": "Late in the Makkan years, sorrow gathered around the Prophet ﷺ. Khadijah, who had sheltered his heart, died; Abu Talib, who had sheltered his life, died; and Ta’if answered his message with stones. It was after this season of grief, in the year the sira calls the Year of Sorrow, that Allah carried His servant by night from the Sacred Mosque to the Farthest Mosque and raised him through the seven heavens, brought nearer than any created being had been brought, and returned him before dawn carrying the gift his people still hold: the prayer.", "anchor": {"text": "The Isra itself, Masjid al-Haram to Masjid al-Aqsa, is stated by the Qur'an", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur'an 17:1"]}}, "chapters": [{"n": 1, "title": "The Year of Sorrow", "body": "Before the ascent came the breaking. Within a short span the Prophet ﷺ lost Khadijah, the first believer, the wife who had wrapped him in his trembling and spent her wealth on his mission, and Abu Talib, the uncle whose standing had held Quraysh away from him. When he walked to Ta’if seeking shelter for the message, its people set their fools on him until his sandals ran with blood. The sira remembers it as the Year of Sorrow. Heaven answered that grief not with ease but with nearness. What follows is the night on which the wounded servant was summoned upward, honored in the company of every prophet, and drawn closer than any created being had ever been drawn.", "level": "mixed", "refs": ["Ibn Hisham, as-Sirah an-Nabawiyyah"], "note": "The name Year of Sorrow and the placing of the Night Journey soon after it rest on sira chronology; no authentic hadith fixes the exact year or calendar date, and any precise date given for this night is later convention, not transmission."}, {"n": 2, "title": "The Chest Was Opened", "body": "The narrations of Anas ibn Malik and Malik ibn Sa’sa’ah open quietly: the Prophet ﷺ lay resting near the House, in the state between sleeping and waking, when Jibril alayhis-salam descended to him. His chest was opened from the pit of the throat to the abdomen, and his heart was taken out and washed with the water of Zamzam. There was no violence in it; this was purification, the preparation of a vessel rather than a wound. A servant was about to be carried where nothing of earth could go with him. Before the body was raised through the heavens, the heart was made ready to bear what the heavens would show it, and to carry back down what it would be given.", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 3887", "Muslim 162", "Bukhari 349"], "note": "An authentic narration in Sahih Muslim records an earlier opening of his chest in childhood; scholars read the washing on this night as a preparation specific to the ascent."}, {"n": 3, "title": "The Vessel of Wisdom and Faith", "body": "Then a basin of gold was brought, filled with wisdom and faith, and it was emptied into his chest before the chest was closed. The narrators do not explain how faith can fill a vessel, and the Prophet ﷺ did not elaborate; the report is left in its strangeness, as revelation sometimes is. What the night would ask of him was not courage alone but certainty, the settled knowledge that what the eye was about to see was true and from his Lord. Wisdom and faith were poured into him the way provision is given to a traveler, measured for the road ahead: seven heavens, the prophets in their stations, the Lote Tree at the limit of things, and words spoken beyond it.", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 3887", "Bukhari 349", "Muslim 162"], "note": ""}, {"n": 4, "title": "The Arrival of al-Buraq", "body": "He was brought a mount whom the narrations call al-Buraq: a white beast, smaller than a mule and larger than a donkey, placing its hoof at the farthest point its sight could reach. The authentic reports say nothing more of its form, and restraint honors them; whatever later storytelling added belongs to imagination, not transmission. Distance, for such a creature, was a matter of glances. The Prophet ﷺ mounted, with Jibril beside him, and the road to Jerusalem shrank beneath that stride. In the narration of Anas he tied al-Buraq at the ring by the gate of the sanctuary, the same ring, he said, where the prophets before him had tied it, one small gesture binding this journey to every journey heaven had ever commanded.", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 3887", "Muslim 162"], "note": ""}, {"n": 5, "title": "From the Sacred Mosque to the Farthest Mosque", "body": "The Qur’an itself carries this stage of the journey, in words recited in every generation since: Glorified is He who took His servant by night from the Sacred Mosque to the Farthest Mosque, whose surroundings We have blessed, that We might show him of Our signs. Each word is weighed. It happened by night, in secrecy and stillness; he traveled as His servant, the title of deepest nearness; and the road ran first to al-Aqsa, sanctuary of the earlier prophets, before it turned upward. Makkah and Jerusalem were joined in a single night, and this ummah has understood ever since that its story does not begin with itself. The Prophet ﷺ entered a line of prophecy older than empires, and prayed where his brothers had prayed.", "level": "quran", "refs": ["Qur'an 17:1", "Muslim 162"], "note": "The majority of scholars hold the journey took place with body and soul together, awake, which is why Quraysh found the claim so enormous; a minority of early voices spoke of vision, and the commentaries record the discussion."}, {"n": 6, "title": "The Prayer of the Prophets", "body": "At Bayt al-Maqdis he entered the sanctuary and prayed two rak’ahs. In the narration of Abu Hurayrah, the time of prayer came and he led them, the assembled company of prophets gathered in rows behind one imam from the descendants of Isma’il alayhis-salam. The reports linger on no ceremony; the scene is given in a line, as if its meaning needed no help. Every messenger had carried the one call through his own people and his own age; on this night the callers themselves stood shoulder to shoulder, and the Prophet ﷺ of the final nation stood in front. Leadership in prayer is the quietest of honors, and it was granted quietly, in the sanctuary that had held prophecy for centuries.", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Muslim 162", "Muslim 172"], "note": "Scholars differ over whether this congregational prayer occurred before the ascent or after the return; the transmitted wordings admit both arrangements."}, {"n": 7, "title": "The Choice Between Milk and Wine", "body": "Two vessels were set before him, one of milk and one of wine. He took the milk, and Jibril said: praise be to Allah who guided you to the fitrah; had you taken the wine, your ummah would have gone astray. The test was placed at the hinge of the journey, before the heavens opened. Milk is the first food of every human life, nourishment without confusion, sweetness that clarifies; wine is sweetness that unseats the mind. The choice was made once, on behalf of everyone who would afterward belong to him. In the Prophet’s ﷺ steady hand reaching for the plainer cup, the temper of the final religion was quietly fixed: a way aligned with how human beings are made.", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Muslim 162", "Bukhari 5576"], "note": "Bukhari 3887 places offered vessels, wine, milk, and in some wordings honey, at Sidrat al-Muntaha, while Bukhari 5576 sets two vessels at Bayt al-Maqdis; scholars allow that both offerings occurred."}, {"n": 8, "title": "The First Gate of Heaven", "body": "Then the ascent, the Mi’raj, began. At the lowest heaven Jibril asked for the gate to be opened, and a voice asked: who is this? Jibril. And who is with you? Muhammad ﷺ. Has he been sent for? He has. Then the gate was opened, and the keeper said: welcome to him, and what an excellent coming is his. The exchange repeats at every heaven, and the narrations preserve it each time without shortening it, seven questions and seven welcomes. Heaven does not open by right of arrival but by invitation; even the best of creation entered as one sent for. Above the first gate the unseen world stood arranged in its ranks, waiting to receive its guest.", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 3887", "Muslim 164"], "note": ""}, {"n": 9, "title": "Adam and the Two Directions of Humanity", "body": "In the first heaven sat Adam alayhis-salam, the father of humankind. He greeted the Prophet ﷺ as a righteous son and a righteous prophet. In the narration of Abu Dharr, multitudes of souls were arrayed on his right and on his left; when he looked to his right he smiled, and when he looked to his left he wept. These were the souls of his descendants: the people of Paradise on the one hand, the people of the Fire on the other. The first ancestor still watches the long procession of his children, rejoicing and grieving by turns. No scene in the ascent is more human than this. The journey through the heavens begins with a father’s face, moved by what becomes of his family.", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 3887", "Bukhari 349"], "note": ""}, {"n": 10, "title": "Yahya and Isa", "body": "In the second heaven he met the two maternal cousins, Yahya alayhis-salam and Isa alayhis-salam, son of Zakariyya alayhis-salam and son of Maryam. Each welcomed him as a righteous brother and a righteous prophet. The narrations pause on nothing further, yet the pairing carries weight of its own: two prophets of the house of Imran, one slain unjustly, one denied and then raised beyond reach, now standing together in peace above the world that refused them. For the Prophet ﷺ, walking out of his own years of rejection, the second heaven held a quiet reassurance. Those wronged for the sake of the message do not end inside the wrong done to them; they end in welcome, in company, in light.", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 3887", "Muslim 164"], "note": ""}, {"n": 11, "title": "Yusuf and Beauty", "body": "In the third heaven stood Yusuf alayhis-salam. In the narration of Anas the Prophet ﷺ said of him: he had been given half of all beauty. The line is spoken plainly, without envy and without explanation. Yusuf’s beauty had once been his trial, the cause of the pit, the caravan, and the prison; here it stands transfigured, a mark of honor in a station of honor. The tradition understands his portion as half of beauty itself, and understands too that the fairest of faces was matched by the purest patience. He welcomed the Prophet ﷺ as a brother and a prophet and prayed for his good. What the world once sold at a low price, heaven displays at its true worth.", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 3887", "Muslim 162"], "note": ""}, {"n": 12, "title": "Idris and the High Station", "body": "In the fourth heaven he met Idris alayhis-salam, and the narration itself reaches for the Qur’an to describe him: We raised him to a high station. Little is transmitted of Idris beyond truthfulness and prophethood, and the ascent adds no legend to him; it simply shows the verse fulfilled, a man raised, in the plainest sense, into the heights. There is a lesson in the economy of it. Some of Allah’s servants are known through volumes; some are summarized in a single verse and a single greeting. Idris welcomed the Prophet ﷺ as the others had, righteous brother, righteous prophet, and the travelers passed on upward, through a station whose name the Qur’an had given before the night confirmed it.", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 3887", "Qur'an 19:57"], "note": "Some transmissions vary which heaven a given prophet occupies; this sequence follows the narration of Malik ibn Sa’sa’ah shared by Bukhari and Muslim."}, {"n": 13, "title": "Harun", "body": "In the fifth heaven was Harun alayhis-salam, brother of Musa alayhis-salam and the voice that had steadied a nation while its deliverer stood on the mountain. He greeted the Prophet ﷺ with the words of welcome the others had used, a righteous brother and a righteous prophet. The narrations grant him no speech beyond the greeting, and the silence suits him; his whole prophethood had been a ministry of support, eloquence placed in the service of another man’s mission. In the ordering of the heavens he stands one level beneath his brother, near him beyond the world as he was within it. For a Prophet ﷺ who would soon lean hard on Musa’s counsel, meeting the helper just before the counselor carries its own gentle fitness.", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 3887", "Muslim 164"], "note": ""}, {"n": 14, "title": "Musa", "body": "In the sixth heaven he met Musa, and here the narration slows. When the Prophet ﷺ moved past him toward the seventh heaven, Musa wept. Asked why, he answered: I weep because a young man was sent after me whose ummah will enter Paradise in greater numbers than mine. The scholars read those tears with care: not envy, which has no life in that world, but the sorrow of a shepherd who wanted more for his own flock. Musa had carried Banu Isra’il through sea, desert, and forty years of wandering, and he loved them still. His weeping honors both nations at once. No one in the heavens will engage the Prophet ﷺ more than Musa, and the tears are only the first sign of his care.", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 3887", "Muslim 164"], "note": ""}, {"n": 15, "title": "Ibrahim and the Frequented House", "body": "In the seventh heaven, nearest the boundary of created things, he found Ibrahim alayhis-salam, the intimate friend of Allah, reclining against al-Bayt al-Ma’mur, the Frequented House. Ibrahim greeted him as a righteous son and a righteous prophet, the words Adam had spoken at the first heaven, so that the ascent is framed by two fathers: the father of humankind at its floor, the father of prophets at its summit. The House he leaned against stands above the heavens as the Ka’bah stands upon the earth, one axis of worship running through all the worlds. The man who had raised the foundations of the earthly House now rests beside its heavenly likeness, and between the two Houses the whole religion is strung like a single thread.", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 3887", "Muslim 162"], "note": ""}, {"n": 16, "title": "The Seventy Thousand Who Never Return", "body": "Of al-Bayt al-Ma’mur he was told: every day seventy thousand angels enter it, and those who leave never return to it; it is the last they will ever see of it. The number is not ornament, it is a window onto the scale of the unseen. Creation is vaster than any census, and its worship never repeats. Every day a new host, every day a farewell, from before Adam until the ending of days, and no angel comes twice. On earth, worshippers circle the Ka’bah and come back year after year; the heavens are so thick with servants that a single visit is a lifetime’s share. The report leaves the mind at the edge of a number it cannot see over, which is where minds belong before the unseen.", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 3887", "Muslim 162"], "note": ""}, {"n": 17, "title": "Sidrat al-Muntaha", "body": "Then he was raised to Sidrat al-Muntaha, the Lote Tree of the farthest limit, which the Qur’an names as the station of a second encounter with revelation. The narration reaches for the largest familiar things to describe it: fruits like the great jars of Hajar, leaves like the ears of elephants. From its base issued four rivers, two hidden and two apparent; the hidden pair, he was told, are rivers of Paradise, and the apparent pair are named as the Nile and the Euphrates. Here the knowledge of every created being finds its ceiling; what rises from below ends at this tree, and what descends from above is received at it. Then it was covered by that which covered it, and it changed, until none of Allah’s creation could have described its beauty.", "level": "mixed", "refs": ["Qur'an 53:13-18", "Bukhari 3887", "Muslim 162"], "note": "Commentators, an-Nawawi among them, read the naming of the Nile and the Euphrates as a statement of the blessing attached to their lands rather than a map of their springs."}, {"n": 18, "title": "What No Created Tongue Can Describe", "body": "What happened at the summit is guarded by the Qur’an’s own reticence: the heart did not lie about what it saw; the sight neither swerved nor overreached. Here the first generations themselves differed, and honesty requires saying so. Aisha, the Mother of the Believers, held that whoever claims the Prophet ﷺ saw his Lord has erred gravely; what he saw in his true form was Jibril, and the verses of an-Najm describe that vision. Ibn Abbas held that he saw Him, and in one wording, saw Him with his heart, twice. Later scholars often joined the two: a vision of the heart affirmed, a vision of the eyes denied, and the essence of Allah unseen by anyone in this life. The summit of the ascent stays, fittingly, beyond created description.", "level": "debated", "refs": ["Qur'an 53:1-18", "Muslim 176", "Muslim 177"], "note": "Both positions are preserved in Sahih Muslim; the disagreement concerns the vision at the summit, never the reality of the ascent itself."}, {"n": 19, "title": "Paradise and Kawthar", "body": "He was shown Paradise. The narration of Malik ibn Sa’sa’ah gives two details and stops: domes of pearl, and soil of musk. In another authentic report the Prophet ﷺ said: while I was walking in Paradise I came upon a river whose banks were vaults of hollowed pearl, and I asked Jibril, what is this? He said: this is al-Kawthar, which your Lord has given you. The shortest surah of the Qur’an had promised that abundance in a single breath; the night showed it flowing. The descriptions end early, and rightly. Of the home of the righteous we are handed the scent of its earth, the curve of one riverbank, and the certainty that it is real, prepared, and waiting.", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 3887", "Bukhari 4964", "Qur'an 108:1"], "note": ""}, {"n": 20, "title": "Warnings Shown in the Unseen", "body": "The journey also carried warnings. The strongest of these reports concerns the backbiters: the Prophet ﷺ passed a people with nails of copper, tearing at their own faces and chests, and was told: these are the ones who ate the flesh of people and assaulted their honor. Abu Dawud transmits it with a sound chain. Other punishment scenes in circulation, the devourers of usury, the neglecters of prayer, and more, rest on narrations of varying strength, and several belong to a separate, authentic dream of the Prophet ﷺ rather than to this night; popular retellings have braided the two together. Precision here is itself reverence. What stands firm is the emphasis: on the night of nearness, sins of the tongue against absent people were singled out for horror.", "level": "mixed", "refs": ["Abu Dawud 4878"], "note": "The backbiters scene is Abu Dawud 4878 and is graded strong; several widely retold scenes come instead from the separate dream narration in Bukhari 7047 or from weak chains, and each must be weighed on its own."}, {"n": 21, "title": "Fifty Prayers", "body": "Above the Lote Tree the obligation was given: fifty prayers in every day and night. The Prophet ﷺ accepted and turned homeward. There was no bargaining at the summit; the servant received what his Lord ordained, and the number itself declared how much of a human life is owed to remembrance. Fifty prayers would have threaded worship through every waking hour, a life with no seam between devotion and the rest. That, in truth, is the scale of the debt. What follows in the story is not a lightening of that truth but a mercy in its accounting, and like every mercy of this night it arrives through love: the love of an old prophet for a nation he had never met.", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 3887", "Muslim 162"], "note": ""}, {"n": 22, "title": "The Counsel of Musa", "body": "Descending, he passed Musa, who asked: what were you commanded? Fifty prayers each day, he said. Musa answered: your ummah will not manage it; I tried people before you, and I treated Banu Isra’il with the hardest treatment; return to your Lord and ask Him to lighten it for your ummah. So the Prophet ﷺ returned, and a portion was removed. Musa sent him back, and back again, the narration patient with every repetition, until five remained. The scene honors both men: the elder prophet spending his hard experience on a nation not his own, and the final Prophet ﷺ unashamed to stand before his Lord again and again as a pleader for his people. Between Musa’s realism and Muhammad’s ﷺ asking, the shape of the ummah’s day was settled.", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 3887", "Muslim 162"], "note": "The narrations differ on whether the reductions came ten at a time or five at a time; the endpoint of five is agreed upon in every sound wording."}, {"n": 23, "title": "Five Prayers with the Reward of Fifty", "body": "When five remained, Musa urged him to ask once more. The Prophet ﷺ answered: I have asked my Lord until I am shy before Him; I am content, and I submit. Then a caller announced: I have carried through My obligation and lightened the burden on My servants; they are five, and they are fifty; the word is not changed with Me. Five in performance, fifty in reward, each prayer weighed at ten, the arithmetic of a generosity that had intended mercy from the first. The final number was not wrung from heaven; it was a gift heaven had prepared, given in a way that let one prophet counsel, another plead, and a people know forever how they were loved. He came down carrying the religion’s daily heartbeat.", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 3887", "Muslim 162"], "note": ""}, {"n": 24, "title": "The Return Before Dawn", "body": "He was returned to Makkah before dawn had broken over it, the whole immensity folded into a fraction of one night. The authentic narrations spend no words on the homeward road; a journey that outran description outran its narrators too. Morning found the Prophet ﷺ among his people carrying the heaviest of tidings: that he had been, in a single night, to Jerusalem and beyond the seventh heaven. The sira adds that he spoke of caravans passed on the way and the hour of their arrival, details his hearers could test; those reports vary in strength and are read as supporting detail, not foundation. The foundation was starker: one truthful man’s word against the whole weight of the possible.", "level": "mixed", "refs": ["Bukhari 3886", "Muslim 170"], "note": ""}, {"n": 25, "title": "The Test of Al-Aqsa", "body": "By morning the claim was in the open, and Quraysh received it as a gift, the perfect absurdity with which to finish him. Men who had ridden a month to reach Syria knew what one night meant. Those who had seen Jerusalem pressed him to describe it. The Prophet ﷺ later told what happened: when Quraysh called me a liar, I stood in al-Hijr, and Allah displayed Bayt al-Maqdis before me, and I began to tell them of its signs while I was looking at it. He had crossed it by night, his gaze set on higher things; the examiners wanted its doors and windows. So the sanctuary was raised before his eyes, and he answered from the original. The test built to break the story became part of its proof.", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 3886", "Muslim 170"], "note": ""}, {"n": 26, "title": "Abu Bakr As-Siddiq", "body": "The report reached Abu Bakr as a taunt: your companion claims he went by night to Bayt al-Maqdis and returned before morning. His answer, preserved in the report of al-Hakim and others, wasted no breath on geography: if he said it, then he has spoken the truth. And he went further: I believe him in what is more distant than that; I believe him about news from heaven arriving in a morning or an evening. From that day, the tradition holds, he was called as-Siddiq, the ever-affirming. The grading of the naming report is discussed by the scholars, and its meaning is beyond dispute in the memory of the ummah. Faith of this kind does not verify claims one by one; it has already verified the man.", "level": "mixed", "refs": ["Bukhari 3886", "al-Hakim, al-Mustadrak"], "note": "The naming report is in al-Hakim’s Mustadrak, graded sahih by him with adh-Dhahabi concurring; it is not in Bukhari or Muslim, though the disbelief of Quraysh that occasioned it is."}, {"n": 27, "title": "The Gift Carried by Every Muslim", "body": "Every other ordinance came down to the earth; the prayer alone was given at the summit, received without any intermediary space between the Giver and the one receiving. That is its measure. In the narration of Ibn Mas’ud, three things were given that night: the five prayers, the closing verses of Surat al-Baqarah, and the promise of forgiveness for whoever meets Allah without associating anything with Him. Of the whole journey, the heavens, the Tree, the rivers, the welcomes, this is what the Prophet ﷺ carried home. Fourteen centuries on, a tired worker rising before first light to pray fajr is holding the night’s gift. Five times a day, in every land on earth, some part of this night returns: a believer turns toward the qiblah, and the road opened in the Year of Sorrow opens again.", "level": "sunnah", "refs": ["Bukhari 3887", "Muslim 162", "Muslim 173"], "note": ""}]}};
